id: ops-cxrx
title: "Four more fail modes in the canonical gate: an arm pattern read as an opener, an escaped run key read as data, a sequence marker missing from the indent, and composite actions unscanned"
description: "The twelfth through fifteenth fail modes found in this gate, all reported by review on this branch and all confirmed by executing the auditor.\n\nAn arm pattern carrying the optional leading parenthesis was consumed as a balanced group, so the scan found the nested block's arm label instead of the enclosing one and skipped the sibling reset. A publish in the nested block then inherited the previous arm's binding. Two reviewers reported this independently; only the parenthesised spelling reproduced, because the bare spelling was already refused by another path.\n\nA double-quoted YAML key carrying an escape was compared to the word run as raw text, so an escaped spelling of run was classified as data and its body never reached the shell scan. In isolation that merely made the scan vacuous, which fails closed. Composed with a single attested sibling the same construction returned no failures at all, which is the shape this gate keeps failing in and the reason a finding must be judged composed rather than alone.\n\nThe block scalar indentation indicator counts from the parent node, and in a sequence entry the item marker is part of that node's indentation. Omitting its width stripped two columns too few and left residual indentation, which is enough to stop a heredoc terminator matching at the shell line start. Settled against a real YAML parser rather than argued from the specification, which also showed that the existing guard for this construction was asserting against a document no parser accepts.\n\nA composite action is recognised by its file name wherever it sits, so a repository that is itself an action carries the definition at its root. Matching only the actions subdirectory left those shell blocks unscanned."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: "area:gates","area:supply-chain","type:security"
created_at: "2026-09-02T19:24:37.016Z"
